[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66019,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Alveolar soft part sarcoma (ASPS) is a rare tumour of uncertain histogenesis predominantly affecting the deep soft tissues of the extremities, featuring variably discohesive epithelioid cells arranged in nests, resulting in a distinctive alveolar appearance. It is characterized by a specific translocation, der(17)t(X;17)(p11.2;q25), which results in <em>ASPSCR1</em>-<em>TFE3</em> gene fusion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66020,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9581/3 Alveolar soft part sarcoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66021,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B5F.2 &amp; XH8V95 Sarcoma, not elsewhere classified of other specified sites &amp; Alveolar soft part sarcoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66022,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66023,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66024,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common sites in the genital tract include the uterine corpus and cervix, vagina, and vulva.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66025,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with nonspecific signs and symptoms related to a mass involving the uterus, vagina, or vulva {28009610}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66026,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ASPS rarely involves the female genital tract. It most frequently occurs in patients in their second to fourth decade of life {28009610}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66027,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66028,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ASPS is characterized by an unbalanced der(17)t(X;17)(p11.2;q25) resulting in fusion of <em>ASPSCR1</em> and <em>TFE3</em> {11244503}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66029,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours have a yellow to tan or grey cut surface with occasional haemorrhage and/or necrosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66030,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ASPS typically has lobules with nested architecture in which tumour cells fill or occupy the centre of nests, and/or alveolar architecture in which there is loss of central tumour cell cohesion resulting in unoccupied spaces. Some tumours show pure sheet-like growth. In lobulated tumours, a prominent sinusoidal vasculature surrounds individual nests. Extracellular hyalinization can be present, usually focally. Classically, the cells are dyscohesive and uniformly polygonal, with ample granular eosinophilic cytoplasm and central or eccentrically located round nuclei with prominent nucleoli. A small subset of cells have extensive clear cytoplasm. Mitotic activity is infrequent {28009610; 8598329}. Some tumours have intracytoplasmic rectangular or rhomboid crystals that are highlighted by PASD. Immunohistochemically, TFE3 is diffusely and strongly positive in all tumours. Rare cases express muscle markers or focally express HMB45 {28009610; 8598329}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66031,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In cytological preparations, smears reveal large cells, mainly distributed singly, with granularity or vacuoles {19787801; 1879268}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66032,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Demonstration of <em>TFE3</em> rearrangement {23715164} or <em>ASPSCR1</em>-<em>TFE3</em> fusion transcripts {30139549; 30459475} may be diagnostically helpful.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66033,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> nested, pseudoalveolar, or sheet-like growth; dyscohesive tumour cells with clear to eosinophilic granular cytoplasm; infrequent mitotic activity.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> TFE3 nuclear expression by immunohistochemistry (strong and diffuse); PAS-positive intracytoplasmic rectangular or rhomboid crystals; identification of der(17)t(X;17)(p11.2;q25) or <em>ASPSCR1</em>-<em>TFE3</em>, in selected cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66034,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ASPS is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) staging system for sarcoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66035,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 442,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ASPS is associated with late recurrence, sometimes decades after diagnosis. Although data are limited, tumours arising in the female genital tract may have a better prognosis than tumours at other sites {28009610; 8598329}. To date, only one reported case arising in the uterine corpus had metastases (to pelvic lymph nodes) {22977670}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:36:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]